Q: How can I read a local text file in the browser? A: <p>Using <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Fetch</a> and an async function:</p>
<pre><code>const logFileText = async file =&gt; {
    const response = await fetch(file)
    const text = await response.text()
    console.log(text)
}

logFileText('file.txt')
</code></pre>

Q: Google Web Toolkit - Flextable CSS A: <p>Try with CSS style that is more manageable. <em>If you want to change it in future then you don't need to touch the JAVA code.</em></p>

<p><strong>In this way you can benefit of Themes.</strong></p>

<p>JAVA:</p>

<pre><code>// set style for first row and first column
flexTable.getFlexCellFormatter().setStyleName(0, 0,"yellowBackground");
</code></pre>

<p>CSS:</p>

<pre><code>.yellowBackground {
    background-color: yellow;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to reliably and effectively check domain availability in batch with Node.js? A: <p>If you are looking for something that has no rate limiting and is free, you can use the Cloudflare's public DNS resolver (1.1.1.1) for checking domain availability. Example:</p>
<pre><code>#!/usr/bin/env node

const dns = require('dns').promises;
dns.setServers(['1.1.1.1']);


const DOMAIN_TO_CHECK = &quot;example.com&quot;;  


async function checkDomainAvailability(domain) {
  domain = domain
    .replace(/^https?:\/\//, '')
    .replace(/\/$/, '')
    .trim();

  console.log(`Checking: ${domain}`);

  try {
    await dns.resolve4(domain);
    console.log(`❌ ${domain} is TAKEN`);
    return false;
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.code === 'ENOTFOUND') {
      console.log(`✅ ${domain} appears to be AVAILABLE`);
      return true;
    }

    console.log(`⚠️  Unexpected DNS error for ${domain}: ${err.code || err.message}`);
    return null; // uncertain
  }
}

const DOMAINS = [
  &quot;example.com&quot;,
  &quot;kattklubben.nu&quot;,
  &quot;xai-2026.com&quot;,
  &quot;grok6.org&quot;
];

(async () =&gt; {
  for (const domain of DOMAINS) {
    await checkDomainAvailability(domain);
    console.log('─'.repeat(50));
  }
})();
</code></pre>
<p>However, just checking DNS will give inaccurate results for some domains (some domains are registered but have no DNS records). A much better approach: check DNS first, and only fall back to WHOIS/RDAP if no records are found. Since WHOIS checks are rate limited, it's a good idea to check the DNS first, this way you don't need to check WHOIS for every single domain check. <strong>This will work for most domains, but domains that are blocked or reserved by the registry (= unavailable), will sometimes show up as available with this method.</strong></p>
<p>If you want high accuracy, you can use a service such as the <a href="https://www.namecheap.com/support/api/intro/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Namecheap API</a> or <a href="https://api.domainduck.io/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Domainduck API</a>, which will give accurate availability data for most reserved domain names. Also, these APIs can also show if the domain is listed on a domain aftermarket/marketplace.</p>
